<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arachosia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arachosia was an ancient region in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, centred around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. It was an Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Around 303 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, as part of the settlement that shaped the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arachosia.jpg" alt="Arachosia" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThreeDottedDomino · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Arachosia_around_500_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His coins have been found in <b>Arachosia</b> and the Kabul valley; he conquered those regions and perhaps Gandhara, though he struck no Indian coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with Seleucus I Nicator around 305–303 BCE, he gained <b>Arachosia</b>, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern Pakistan and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Indian territories after the Macedonian withdrawal; the resulting Treaty of the Indus ceded the eastern satrapies of Paropamisadae, <b>Arachosia</b> and Gedrosia, in whole or in part, to the Mauryas in exchange for 500 war elephants, which proved decisive at the Battle of Ipsus in 301 BCE, where Seleucus…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gondophares, a ruler of Iranian, probably Parthian, origin from Sistan, built a kingdom in the early 1st century by taking <b>Arachosia</b> and then Gandhara and Taxila from the Indo-Scythians.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>They passed through Sistan and <b>Arachosia</b> into the Indus valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
